            {
              "id": "86cd1d6b-c237-47a4-8259-7a8a8eebae39",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": "<em><strong>The Grasshopper: Games, Life, &amp; Utopia</strong></em> was written by <a href=\"#/bernard-suits\">Bernard Suits</a>, originally published by University of Toronto Press, January 1, 1978."
            },
            {
              "id": "0643e859-636c-4efa-aec3-2f3f44c08e92",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": ""
            },
            {
              "id": "9a200b0e-acf8-48db-adbc-4e2763d33c49",
              "type": "quote",
              "html": "<em>In the mid twentieth century the philosopher Ludwig Wittgenstein famously asserted that games are indefinable; there are no common threads that link them all. “Nonsense,” said the sensible Bernard Suits: “playing a game is a voluntary attempt to overcome unnecessary obstacles.” The short book Suits wrote demonstrating precisely that is as playful as it is insightful, as stimulating as it is delightful. Through the jocular voice of Aesop's Grasshopper, a “shiftless but thoughtful practitioner of applied entomology,” Suits not only argues that games can be meaningfully defined; he also suggests that playing games is a central part of the ideal of human existence, and so games belong at the heart of any vision of Utopia.</em>"
            },
            {
              "id": "34373bcc-4b23-4e7a-8d9f-6aaaade47f7f",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": ""
            }
          ]
